1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,344
The Periodic Table arranges elements in rows called periods and columns called groups.

2
00:00:05,344 --> 00:00:10,442
Elements in the same group have the same number of electrons in their outer shell,

3
00:00:10,442 --> 00:00:12,960
so they have similar chemical properties.

4
00:00:14,022 --> 00:00:18,198
Group 1, the alkali metals, includes lithium, sodium and potassium.

5
00:00:18,198 --> 00:00:21,945
Lithium is 2, 1; sodium is 2, 8, 1; potassium is 2, 8, 8, 1.

6
00:00:21,945 --> 00:00:26,490
Each has just one outer electron, and the atoms get bigger down the group.

7
00:00:27,552 --> 00:00:34,001
Small pieces of lithium, sodium and potassium are dropped into troughs of water, behind a safety screen.

8
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:37,994
Predict: which metal will react most violently? Pause and decide.

9
00:00:39,055 --> 00:00:44,276
Watch. Lithium floats and fizzes gently. Sodium whizzes around and melts into a ball.

10
00:00:45,337 --> 00:00:50,190
Potassium reacts so strongly that the hydrogen catches fire with a lilac flame.

11
00:00:51,251 --> 00:00:57,701
Explain what you saw. All three have one electron in their outer shell, which they lose when they react.

12
00:00:57,701 --> 00:01:00,342
Going down the group the atoms are bigger,

13
00:01:00,342 --> 00:01:04,887
so the outer electron is further from the nucleus and held less strongly.

14
00:01:04,887 --> 00:01:10,968
It is lost more easily, so potassium reacts most violently. Pause and explain it in your own words.

15
00:01:12,029 --> 00:01:16,636
Alkali metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas.

16
00:01:16,636 --> 00:01:20,813
For sodium: sodium plus water gives sodium hydroxide plus hydrogen.

17
00:01:20,813 --> 00:01:25,542
Metal hydroxides are alkaline, which is why they're called the alkali metals.

18
00:01:26,604 --> 00:01:30,350
Look at the trend: lithium fizzes gently, sodium vigorously,

19
00:01:30,350 --> 00:01:33,422
and potassium so strongly it makes a lilac flame.

20
00:01:33,422 --> 00:01:38,274
Reactivity increases down the group, while melting and boiling points decrease.

21
00:01:39,335 --> 00:01:43,144
These are the melting points of the first five alkali metals,

22
00:01:43,144 --> 00:01:46,399
from the Royal Society of Chemistry's Periodic Table.

23
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:52,251
Lithium melts at about 181 degrees Celsius, sodium at 98 and potassium at 64.

24
00:01:52,251 --> 00:01:57,718
Rubidium melts at 39, and caesium at only 28.5 degrees: it would melt on a very warm day.

25
00:01:58,779 --> 00:02:02,895
Pause and decide: what is the trend in melting point down Group 1,

26
00:02:02,895 --> 00:02:06,457
and what would you predict for francium, the next element?

27
00:02:07,519 --> 00:02:13,231
Now it is your turn to shine! Pause the video after each question and try to answer it first.

28
00:02:14,292 --> 00:02:18,776
Question 1: What particles are found in the nucleus? Pause the video now.

29
00:02:19,838 --> 00:02:21,619
Answer: Protons and neutrons.

30
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:28,393
Question 2: An atom has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. How many neutrons does it have?

31
00:02:28,393 --> 00:02:29,621
Pause the video now.

32
00:02:30,682 --> 00:02:31,911
Answer: 12 neutrons.

33
00:02:32,972 --> 00:02:37,825
Question 3: What is the electronic structure of magnesium? Pause the video now.

34
00:02:38,886 --> 00:02:39,869
Answer: 2, 8, 2.

35
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:45,783
Question 4: What happens to reactivity going down Group 1? Pause the video now.

36
00:02:46,844 --> 00:02:48,134
Answer: It increases.
